---
// Wovyr marketing landing — the site root (/). Docs keep their section routes.
// Ported from website/landing/prototypes/wovyr-landing-v2.html (reviewed).
//
// Identity: the plate-atlas system — a warp grid the whole page is woven onto,
// numbered plates, indigo and madder dyes, and a wolf's head woven from weft
// threads of light. The figure has no outline and no eyes by design: the weft
// rows stop exactly where the silhouette stops, so the shape is read from where
// the threads end. See website/landing/DESIGN-system.md.
//
// Preload the three faces actually used above the fold (Archivo variable, and
// IBM Plex Mono 400/500) so the display face isn't waiting on CSSOM discovery —
// the same reasoning as the retired page's JetBrains Mono preload.
// WEB-305: the face declarations are imported HERE, not via
// `@import "../styles/landing-fonts.css"` inside the <style is:global> block
// below, which is where they used to live. Vite does not resolve a
// node_modules stylesheet's own relative `url(./files/…)` when it is reached
// through a CSS @import chain nested in an Astro component style — the built
// bundle kept the literal `files/archivo-latin-wght-normal.woff2`, which
// resolves against /_astro/ and 404s. Every @font-face on the page failed, so
// the whole plate-atlas identity (DESIGN-system.md §3) silently rendered in
// system-ui and a system mono, on production, while the hashed font files sat
// in /_astro/ unreferenced. As a frontmatter import it is a real Vite entry
// and the URLs are rewritten to the hashed emitted assets — the same reason
// the docs' fonts.css works through Starlight's `customCss`.
import '../styles/landing-fonts.css';

import archivoUrl from '@fontsource-variable/archivo/files/archivo-latin-wght-normal.woff2?url';
import monoRegularUrl from '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/files/ibm-plex-mono-latin-400-normal.woff2?url';
import monoMediumUrl from '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/files/ibm-plex-mono-latin-500-normal.woff2?url';

const CONTACT = 'contact@wovyr.com';

const SITE = 'https://wovyr.com';
const REPO = 'https://github.com/punarduttrajput/wovyr';
const OG_IMAGE = `${SITE}/og.png`;
// The <h1> stays the figurative line the whole design system is built on; the
// title and description are the surfaces a reader meets *without* the page —
// a search result, a shared link, an answer engine's summary — so they carry
// the plain claim instead. Same product, stated from the buyer's side.
const TITLE = 'Wovyr — Policy and an audit trail for AI-generated interfaces';
const DESCRIPTION =
  'Wovyr checks every interface your AI agents generate against policy before it renders, refuses what fails, and records the exact frame the user saw in a tamper-evident hash chain — then holds the work until a human decides. Watch it cut a credential form and survive a kill -9 in the 90-second demo. Apache-2.0, self-hosted, one Rust binary.';

/**
 * GEO-103: the FAQ is the source for both the rendered section and the
 * FAQPage JSON-LD below — one array, so the two can never disagree. A
 * structured-data answer that is not present in the visible page is a
 * spam signal, not a ranking one.
 *
 * The questions are the ones actually asked about a project like this
 * (what is it, do I need it, what does it cost, is it production-ready),
 * and the answers stay inside what the repo genuinely ships — including
 * the honest "not yet" on the distributed scheduler. An answer engine
 * will repeat these verbatim; an overclaim here becomes an overclaim in
 * every future answer about Wovyr.
 */
const FAQ = [
  {
    q: 'What is Wovyr?',
    a: 'Wovyr is an open-source trust layer for interfaces that AI agents generate at runtime. An agent composes a screen from a constrained component vocabulary; Wovyr evaluates that frame against policy before anything renders, refuses anything the policy does not recognise, records the frame and the verdict in a tamper-evident hash chain, and durably pauses the run until a human decides. It runs on a complete AI agent operating system written in Rust and ships as a single binary.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do AI-generated interfaces need a trust layer?',
    a: 'Because the assumption that a person reviewed the screen no longer holds. A model can compose a convincing payment form or a mislabelled confirm button for one user, once, and nobody reviewed it. Prompt injection does not have to change the answer to be dangerous — it only has to change the button. And when a user later says the interface told them to approve something, you need the exact frame they saw, which is only possible if something kept it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I watch Wovyr block something before I install it?',
    a: 'Yes. The repository ships a 90-second recorded run with five beats: a poisoned agent composes a checkout frame asking for a card number and policy cuts it; the safe variant presents, is content-hashed and pends on a human; the server is killed mid-flight and resumes on the same frame; an action the frame never declared is refused; and the whole session is re-read from the audit chain and verified link by link. The replay is a self-contained page on this site, and the driver that produced it is also a test — every beat is asserted, so a green run is evidence rather than narration. Reproduce it yourself with node demo/killer-demo.mjs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is Wovyr different from an LLM guardrail or content filter?',
    a: 'Content guardrails inspect text. Wovyr inspects interface structure: which components a frame uses, what inputs it asks for, whether an action label matches the action class behind it, and where its images come from. It is also structurally constrained rather than only filtered — the component vocabulary has no raw-HTML or script node and no credential-input component, so an entire class of generated interface cannot be expressed in the first place.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does "fail-closed" mean in Wovyr?',
    a: 'Anything the policy does not explicitly recognise is denied rather than allowed with a warning. A frame containing a password field is not logged and passed through — it is refused, the refusal is recorded with the rule that fired, and the human never sees the frame. With no policy configured at all, display-only frames pass and interactive frames are denied, so an unconfigured deployment is restrictive rather than permissive.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Wovyr free and open source?',
    a: 'Yes — Apache-2.0, including the patent grant, with no paid edition and no usage limits. The runtime, the trust layer, the dashboard, the client SDKs, the Helm chart and the documentation are one repository. Contributions use a DCO sign-off rather than a CLA, so contributors keep their copyright. All 21 library crates are published on crates.io, so you can depend on just the trust layer (wovyr-ui-guard) without adopting the runtime.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I self-host Wovyr, and does it need an API key?',
    a: 'Wovyr is self-hosted by default and has no hosted service to sign up for. Install the CLI with cargo install wovyr-cli and run wovyr dev to get the whole platform on localhost. No API key is required to try it: a deterministic mock provider answers, so you can watch a full agent and tool loop offline before wiring a real model. Telemetry is sent only if you configure an OTLP endpoint yourself.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which LLM providers does Wovyr support?',
    a: 'OpenAI and any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, Anthropic through the native Messages API with prompt caching, and local models via mistral.rs. The gateway is provider-neutral and adds failover between providers, a circuit breaker, response caching, retries with jitter and per-model cost accounting, so switching or adding a provider does not change calling code.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Wovyr production-ready?',
    a: 'It ships today as a single-node appliance: authentication, TLS, RBAC, multi-tenancy, quotas, KMS envelope encryption, a keyed audit chain, crash-safe durable workflows and backup/restore are implemented and tested. The distributed scheduler and multi-node work queue are tested library code that is not yet wired into the shipped binary. Every documentation page is marked shipped or planned so you can tell which is which before building on it.',
  },
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
  <link rel="preload" href={archivoUrl} as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />
  <link rel="preload" href={monoRegularUrl} as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />
  <link rel="preload" href={monoMediumUrl} as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />
  {/* The same TITLE the og:/twitter: tags use — it was a duplicated literal
      here, which is exactly how a title and its share card drift apart. */}
  <title>{TITLE}</title>
  <meta name="description" content={DESCRIPTION} />
  <link rel="canonical" href={`${SITE}/`} />
  <link rel="sitemap" href="/sitemap-index.xml" />
  {/* Opt into full-length snippets and large image previews; both default to
      something shorter, and a truncated snippet on the one page that explains
      what the project is costs more here than anywhere else on the site. */}
  <meta name="robots" content="index, follow, max-snippet:-1, max-image-preview:large, max-video-preview:-1" />

  {/* SEO-105: og:image was a root-relative `/og.png`. Facebook, LinkedIn and
      Slack's scrapers do not reliably resolve a relative og:image, so the card
      silently fell back to no image on exactly the surfaces the image exists
      for. Absolute URL, with the dimensions declared so a scraper can lay the
      card out before it has fetched the file. */}
  <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="Wovyr" />
  <meta property="og:locale" content="en" />
  <meta property="og:url" content={`${SITE}/`} />
  <meta property="og:title" content={TITLE} />
  <meta property="og:description" content={DESCRIPTION} />
  <meta property="og:image" content={OG_IMAGE} />
  <meta property="og:image:type" content="image/png" />
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
  <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="Wovyr — the trust layer for AI-generated interfaces" />
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
  <meta name="twitter:title" content={TITLE} />
  <meta name="twitter:description" content={DESCRIPTION} />
  <meta name="twitter:image" content={OG_IMAGE} />
  <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="Wovyr — the trust layer for AI-generated interfaces" />

  {/* Matches the docs' Head override, so the browser chrome does not change
      colour when a visitor crosses from `/` into `/00-executive/…`. */}
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#0C1115" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#DEE3E7" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
  <script is:inline>
    /* Set the theme before first paint to avoid a flash, reading the SAME
       "starlight-theme" key the docs site's ThemeProvider.astro uses — this
       page and the docs are one Astro build/origin, so a choice made in
       either surface carries to the other. Starlight stores "" for "follow
       system" and "light"/"dark" for an explicit choice; data-theme is
       always set explicitly here (falling back to matchMedia) because the
       palette below only defines attribute-scoped light/dark blocks.

       The one deliberate exception is the no-JS case: with JS disabled
       data-theme is never set, and the bare :root block — light values — is
       what a visitor sees regardless of OS preference. Content stays fully
       legible either way. */
    try {
      var m = localStorage.getItem("starlight-theme");
      var explicit = (m === "light" || m === "dark") ? m : null;
      var theme = explicit || (matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches ? "dark" : "light");
      document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", theme);
    } catch (e) {
      document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches ? "dark" : "light");
    }
  </script>

<style is:global>
  /* landing-fonts.css is imported from the frontmatter instead — see the note
     there. Reaching it through an @import here breaks every @font-face. */

  /* ================================================================ TOKENS
     Scoped to this page. The canonical cross-surface token file
     (packages/tokens/wovyr-tokens.css) still carries the retired cobalt
     system consumed by the docs and the dashboard — migrating those two
     surfaces is a separate piece of work, flagged in DESIGN-system.md §8.
     Indigo and madder are the two classical dyes: indigo = a sound thread,
     madder = a thread policy cut. Madder is semantic only, never decorative. */
  :root{
    --paper:#DEE3E7; --paper-2:#EBEEF0; --sunk:#D2D9DE;
    --ink:#0E141A; --ink-2:#53616D; --warp:#C3CCD3; --warp-2:#AEB9C2;
    --indigo:#27386B; --indigo-soft:#8FA0CE; --madder:#A63A26;
    --ui:'Archivo Variable',system-ui,-apple-system,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;
    --mono:'IBM Plex Mono',ui-monospace,'Cascadia Mono',Consolas,monospace;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){
    :root{
      --paper:#0C1115; --paper-2:#131920; --sunk:#080B0E;
      --ink:#E7EBEF; --ink-2:#8B98A3; --warp:#1D252C; --warp-2:#2C3641;
      --indigo:#8AA2E8; --indigo-soft:#3C4C7E; --madder:#E5735C;
    }
  }
  :root[data-theme="light"]{
    --paper:#DEE3E7; --paper-2:#EBEEF0; --sunk:#D2D9DE;
    --ink:#0E141A; --ink-2:#53616D; --warp:#C3CCD3; --warp-2:#AEB9C2;
    --indigo:#27386B; --indigo-soft:#8FA0CE; --madder:#A63A26;
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"]{
    --paper:#0C1115; --paper-2:#131920; --sunk:#080B0E;
    --ink:#E7EBEF; --ink-2:#8B98A3; --warp:#1D252C; --warp-2:#2C3641;
    --indigo:#8AA2E8; --indigo-soft:#3C4C7E; --madder:#E5735C;
  }

  /* ================================================================== BASE */
  *{box-sizing:border-box}
  html{scroll-behavior:smooth}
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){html{scroll-behavior:auto}}
  body{
    margin:0; background:var(--paper); color:var(--ink);
    font-family:var(--ui); font-size:16px; line-height:1.65;
    -webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased;
    /* `clip`, not `hidden` — `overflow-x:hidden` here would make body a scroll
       container in its own right (CSS2 coerces the other axis away from
       `visible`), which breaks viewport-relative scroll machinery. */
    overflow-x:clip;
    position:relative;
  }
  /* the warp — vertical threads the whole page is woven onto */
  body::before{
    content:""; position:absolute; inset:0; z-index:0; pointer-events:none;
    background:repeating-linear-gradient(90deg,var(--warp) 0 1px,transparent 1px 46px);
    opacity:.5;
  }
  body > *{position:relative; z-index:1}
  a{color:inherit; text-decoration:none}
  p{margin:0}
  h1,h2,h3{margin:0; font-family:var(--ui); font-weight:700;
    line-height:1.06; letter-spacing:-.021em; text-wrap:balance}
  ::selection{background:var(--indigo); color:var(--paper)}
  :focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--indigo); outline-offset:3px}

  .skip-link{
    position:absolute; top:-100%; left:12px; z-index:100;
    background:var(--paper-2); color:var(--ink); font-family:var(--mono);
    font-size:13px; padding:10px 16px; border:1px solid var(--indigo);
  }
  .skip-link:focus{top:12px}

  .w{max-width:1160px; margin:0 auto; padding-inline:clamp(20px,5vw,54px)}
  .ann{font-family:var(--mono); font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.13em;
    text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--ink-2); font-weight:500}
  .tnum{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
  .sel{border-left:1px dashed var(--warp-2); padding-left:clamp(16px,2.5vw,30px)}

  /* =================================================================== NAV */
  .nb{position:sticky; top:0; z-index:40;
    background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--paper) 90%,transparent);
    backdrop-filter:blur(7px); border-bottom:1px solid var(--warp)}
  .nb-in{display:flex; align-items:center; gap:26px; height:62px}
  .bd{display:flex; align-items:center; gap:10px; font-weight:700; font-size:19px;
    letter-spacing:-.01em}
  .bd svg{width:24px; height:24px; color:var(--indigo); display:block}
  .nl{margin-left:auto; display:flex; gap:24px; align-items:center}
  .nl a{font-family:var(--mono); font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.12em;
    text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--ink-2); padding-block:12px; transition:color .18s}
  .nl a:hover{color:var(--indigo)}
  .tg{width:38px; height:38px; display:grid; place-items:center; cursor:pointer;
    border:1px solid var(--warp-2); background:none; color:var(--ink-2); font-size:12px}
  .tg:hover{color:var(--indigo); border-color:var(--indigo)}
  @media(pointer:coarse){ .tg{width:44px; height:44px} }
  /* WEB-305 raised this from 820px: the bar gained "Demo" and "Partners", and
     at nine items the row starts colliding with the wordmark before the old
     breakpoint ever fires. Collapse earlier rather than let it overlap. */
  @media(max-width:940px){ .nl a:not(.keep){display:none} }

  /* ================================================================== HERO */
  .hero{min-height:min(90svh,900px); display:flex; align-items:center;
    padding-block:clamp(38px,6vw,70px); border-bottom:1px solid var(--warp)}
  .hero-g{display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.18fr) minmax(0,.82fr);
    gap:clamp(28px,5vw,70px); align-items:center; width:100%}
  @media(max-width:900px){ .hero-g{grid-template-columns:1fr} }
  .plate-no{display:flex; align-items:center; gap:12px; margin-bottom:24px}
  .plate-no::after{content:""; flex:1; height:1px; background:var(--warp-2)}
  /* The plain sentence above the figurative headline. A reader who has never
     heard of the product should not have to parse a metaphor to learn what it
     is — but the metaphor is the identity, so this sits *before* it rather
     than replacing it. Deliberately not `.ann`: mono caps at .13em tracking is
     a draftsman's mark, and an 82-character sentence set that way is unreadable. */
  .claim{max-width:54ch; color:var(--ink-2); font-size:.94rem;
    line-height:1.55; margin-bottom:18px}
  .hero h1{font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3.7rem); margin-bottom:22px}
  .hero h1 span{color:var(--indigo)}
  .hero p.sub{max-width:50ch; color:var(--ink-2);
    font-size:clamp(1rem,1.35vw,1.12rem); margin-bottom:30px}
  .acts{display:flex; gap:12px; flex-wrap:wrap}
  .b{font-family:var(--mono); font-size:12px; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:.12em;
    text-transform:uppercase; padding:14px 22px; border:1px solid var(--indigo);
    transition:background .18s,color .18s}
  .b-fill{background:var(--indigo); color:var(--paper)}
  .b-fill:hover{background:transparent; color:var(--indigo)}
  .b-line{color:var(--ink); border-color:var(--warp-2)}
  .b-line:hover{border-color:var(--ink)}

  .figure{position:relative}
  .figure canvas{display:block; width:100%; aspect-ratio:1/1; height:auto}
  .fig-cap{display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top:6px;
    border-top:1px solid var(--warp-2); padding-top:12px}

  /* ================================================================ PLATES */
  .plate{padding-block:clamp(52px,8vw,96px); border-bottom:1px solid var(--warp)}
  .p-head{display:flex; align-items:center; gap:12px; margin-bottom:clamp(24px,4vw,40px)}
  .p-head::after{content:""; flex:1; height:1px; background:var(--warp-2)}
  .plate h2{font-size:clamp(1.8rem,3.4vw,2.7rem); max-width:20ch}
  .plate h2 span{color:var(--indigo)}
  .lede{max-width:62ch; color:var(--ink-2); margin-top:18px}

  .cols{display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr);
    gap:clamp(28px,5vw,68px); align-items:start}
  @media(max-width:880px){ .cols{grid-template-columns:1fr} }

  .notes{display:flex; flex-direction:column}
  .notes > div{padding:19px 0; border-top:1px solid var(--warp)}
  .notes h3{font-size:1.08rem; margin-bottom:6px}
  .notes p{color:var(--ink-2); font-size:.94rem}

  /* ================================================================== FAQ
     GEO-103. Deliberately plain, always-visible prose rather than a
     JS accordion: these answers exist to be read and to be quoted, and
     collapsed-by-default content is both a weaker extraction signal and
     one more thing that breaks with JS off (see WEB-302). Two columns on
     wide viewports only — an answer measured at 90ch is unreadable. */
  .faq{display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));
    gap:0 clamp(28px,5vw,68px); margin-top:clamp(24px,4vw,40px)}
  @media(max-width:880px){ .faq{grid-template-columns:1fr} }
  .faq > div{padding:22px 0; border-top:1px solid var(--warp)}
  .faq h3{font-size:1.06rem; margin-bottom:8px; max-width:34ch}
  .faq p{color:var(--ink-2); font-size:.94rem; max-width:56ch}

  /* A real ordered sequence, so it is numbered. Every segment is indigo —
     madder is reserved for a thread that was actually cut. */
  .pass{display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr); margin-top:clamp(28px,4vw,46px)}
  .pass > div{padding:22px clamp(14px,2vw,26px) 4px 0; border-top:2px solid var(--indigo)}
  @media(max-width:900px){ .pass{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)} }
  @media(max-width:560px){ .pass{grid-template-columns:1fr} }
  .pass .k{margin-bottom:10px}
  .pass h3{font-size:1.14rem; margin-bottom:8px}
  .pass p{color:var(--ink-2); font-size:.92rem}

  .cloth{background:var(--paper-2); border:1px solid var(--warp-2); padding:20px}
  .cloth svg{display:block; width:100%; height:auto}
  .cloth-legend{display:flex; gap:20px; flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top:14px}
  .dot{display:inline-flex; align-items:center; gap:7px}
  .dot i{width:16px; height:2px; display:block}

  .reg{margin-top:clamp(26px,4vw,44px); border-top:1px solid var(--warp-2)}
  .reg > div{display:grid; grid-template-columns:44px minmax(0,.62fr) minmax(0,2fr);
    gap:clamp(10px,2.5vw,26px); padding:17px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--warp);
    align-items:baseline}
  .reg .no{font-family:var(--mono); font-size:11.5px; color:var(--ink-2)}
  .reg .nm{font-weight:600; font-size:1.02rem}
  .reg .ds{color:var(--ink-2); font-size:.93rem}
  @media(max-width:680px){ .reg > div{grid-template-columns:36px 1fr} .reg .ds{grid-column:2} }

  /* Verdict mark on a register row (Plate 05's beats). Madder appears here on
     exactly the two beats where policy cut something, which is the one use §2
     reserves it for — beat 03 kills a process and beat 05 reports an earlier
     block, and neither is itself a cut, so both are indigo. */
  .vd{font-family:var(--mono); font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.13em;
    text-transform:uppercase; font-weight:500; white-space:nowrap; margin-left:10px}
  .vd.cut{color:var(--madder)}
  .vd.snd{color:var(--indigo)}

  .candor{margin-top:22px; background:var(--paper-2); border-left:3px solid var(--madder);
    padding:16px 18px; color:var(--ink-2); font-size:.93rem}
  .candor b{color:var(--ink)}

  /* Colophon — a spec sheet of where the project actually lives. Two ruled
     columns, label left, value right; the value column is mono because every
     entry is a literal you can paste (a package name, a licence id, a repo). */
  .colo{border-top:1px solid var(--warp-2)}
  .colo > div{display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,.5fr) minmax(0,1fr);
    gap:clamp(10px,2.5vw,26px); padding:13px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--warp);
    align-items:baseline}
  .colo dt{font-family:var(--mono); font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.13em;
    text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--ink-2); margin:0}
  .colo dd{margin:0; font-family:var(--mono); font-size:.9rem; color:var(--ink);
    overflow-wrap:anywhere}
  .colo dd a{color:var(--indigo); border-bottom:1px solid var(--warp-2)}
  .colo dd a:hover{border-color:var(--indigo)}
  .colo dd .muted{color:var(--ink-2)}

  .counts{display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(140px,1fr));
    gap:1px; background:var(--warp-2); border:1px solid var(--warp-2);
    margin-top:clamp(22px,4vw,38px)}
  .counts div{background:var(--paper); padding:22px 18px}
  .counts .v{font-size:clamp(1.9rem,3.6vw,2.7rem); font-weight:700; line-height:1;
